WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.820
Kubernetes networking makes service discovery and routing convenient, but connectivity is not authorization.

2
00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:20.680
ClusterIP services, NodePort services, load balancers, ingress controllers, gateways, service meshes, and reverse proxies all shape who can reach a .NET application and through which path.

3
00:00:20.960 --> 00:00:34.400
ASP.NET Core applications behind ingress or reverse proxies need careful review of forwarded headers, HTTPS behavior, secure cookies, redirects, host handling, client IP handling, and health endpoints.

4
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:39.380
The app should understand where TLS terminates and which proxy headers are trusted.

5
00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:49.100
Browser-facing applications still need correct authentication, authorization, CORS, CSRF protections, cookies, and security headers.

6
00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:53.920
Being deployed in Kubernetes does not remove normal web application security requirements.

7
00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:58.660
Internal service-to-service calls also need clear identity and policy expectations.

8
00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:04.860
NetworkPolicies can restrict pod-to-pod traffic when the cluster networking implementation supports them.

9
00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.760
They are most useful when the team understands which workloads need to communicate and can express those paths clearly.

10
00:01:11.860 --> 00:01:18.180
A policy that exists only on paper but is not enforced by the platform does not provide runtime control.

11
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:26.220
Internal admin endpoints, metrics endpoints, debug endpoints, and management paths should not be exposed casually.

12
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:36.480
The production design should identify which endpoints are public, which are internal, which require stronger controls, and which should only be reachable through governed operational channels.
